    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d321">

                <pb facs="0450" n="442" sameAs="frus:frus1941v04/pg442"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 Pacific War/1104</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chief of Naval Operations</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Stark</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-09-11">11 September,
                        1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Op–10 Hu</p>
                <p>You may recall asking me in effect:— <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“What we should do in case the Japs close entrance into the Sea of Japan,
                            thereby shutting off Vladivostok to United States shipping?”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>My feeling is that we should not acquiesce in this action unless Japan and Russia
                    are legally at war.</p>
                <p>Should Japan make a declaration closing entrance to the Sea of Japan I believe we
                    should tell her we refuse to recognize such action, that we should continue to
                    ship goods under the United States flag, <pb facs="0451" n="443" xml:id="pg443"
                    /> and that we should provide naval escort for our flag vessels. Unless she and
                    Russia are legally at war, she has no right whatever to tell us our ships cannot
                    go through the Sea of Japan to Russian ports. I do not know how it could be
                    worked, but if Japan could realize this now perhaps she would not issue any such
                    proclamation.</p>
                <p>If, however, the United States were to decide to acquiesce in such a Japanese
                    declaration, our shipping would need to be diverted to other ports.</p>
                <p>Alternate routes for sea-going vessels are: <list>
                        <label>(<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>)</label>
                        <item>Via Nikolaevsk</item>
                        <label>(<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>)</label>
                        <item>Via Archangel</item>
                        <label>(<hi rendition="#italic">c</hi>)</label>
                        <item>Via Petropavlovsk</item>
                        <label>(<hi rendition="#italic">d</hi>)</label>
                        <item>Via Persian Gulf</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <p>[Here follows a brief analysis of each of the suggested alternate routes.]</p>
                <p>Sec[retary] Knox has read this.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>H[<hi rendition="#small-caps">arold</hi>] E. S[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">tark</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
